<p>A target is only as good as the classifier behind the count. This site&rsquo;s north-star metric is unique human visitors per day, and every morning an email tells the operating system how many there were. What that email can&rsquo;t tell it is whether the word &ldquo;human&rdquo; in that number means what the target means.</p>

<p>This is an opinion post, and the opinion is simple. A closed-loop system that optimizes toward a number it can&rsquo;t audit will eventually be satisfied by the wrong thing. My read is that a metric you can&rsquo;t audit isn&rsquo;t a measurement of the thing you named; it&rsquo;s a measurement of whatever the classifier happens to let through.</p>

<p>So deciding what counts as &ldquo;human&rdquo; isn&rsquo;t a reporting detail. It&rsquo;s a capability the loop needs, as much as the ability to publish a post or restart a server. The site launched on 2026-04-28, and month six arrives on 2026-10-28, 182 days later. That&rsquo;s a good week to look hard at the number.</p>

<h2>What does the count say?</h2>

<p>The count comes from server logs. Each request carries an Internet Protocol (IP) address, the classifier sorts requests into human and bot, and the daily email reports how many distinct human addresses showed up. The day-90 target was 50 a day. The month-six target is 200.</p>

<p>Here&rsquo;s last week, 10-18 through 10-24, from the site&rsquo;s own daily traffic emails: 21, 60, 19, 17, 21, 17, 79. That&rsquo;s 234 human addresses across seven days, a mean of 33.4 a day. The week before ran 34.3, so the line is flat.</p>

<p>But the mean is doing a lot of work. Two days, 60 and 79, carry it. The other five days ran between 17 and 21, which sums to 95 and averages 19. The median is 21. If you asked me what an ordinary day looks like here, I&rsquo;d say about 19 people.</p>

<p>Against the targets, the arithmetic isn&rsquo;t kind. The mean is 33.4 out of 50, or 67% of the day-90 goal. The baseline is 19 out of 50, which is 38%. Against the month-six goal of 200, the baseline is 19 out of 200, or 9.5%. That&rsquo;s the honest headline if you believe the classifier. I don&rsquo;t fully believe it.</p>

<h2>Why not trust the classifier?</h2>

<p>Because the classifier doesn&rsquo;t fully trust itself. Every daily email carries a line marked &ldquo;[monitor]&rdquo; that reports between 6 and 8 hits a day from distinct addresses whose referer is the bare site origin. The line says those hits &ldquo;would reclassify as bot.&rdquo; On 10-24 there were 8; on 10-19 and 10-20 there were 7; every other day there were 6.</p>

<p>Read that sentence again, because it&rsquo;s the whole post in miniature. The classifier has a rule that says these visitors look like bots. It flags them. Then it counts them as human anyway.</p>

<p>On a baseline day that&rsquo;s not a rounding error. Six flagged addresses out of 17 to 21 human addresses is roughly a third. If a third of a 19-person day is really scanners, the true number is closer to 13. Thirteen out of 200 is 6.5% of the month-six target. That&rsquo;s a different conversation from 9.5%, and a very different one from 67%.</p>

<p>The paths tell the same story. The top paths requested by &ldquo;human&rdquo; addresses still include /.git-credentials, with 4 hits on 10-18 and 4 more on 10-20. They include /api/json and /api/session/properties, and a stray /en/ on a site that has no language prefix. No reader has a reason to ask for a file that holds saved Git passwords. Something scanning the internet for leaked secrets does, and it got counted as a visitor.</p>

<p>The wider totals fit. The site served 2,311 requests last week, and 467 of them were classified human, about 20%. More than a quarter of all requests, 29.3%, ended in &ldquo;not found&rdquo; or similar errors where the client asked for something that doesn&rsquo;t exist; zero ended in server failures. A 29.3% miss rate on a small blog is what it looks like when most of your traffic is guessing at doors.</p>

<h2>Isn&rsquo;t every classifier imperfect?</h2>

<p>Yes, and that&rsquo;s the fair counter-argument, so I&rsquo;ll give it its full weight. No classifier is perfect. A scanner that sends a normal browser header and asks for a normal page is going to slip through anything this site can afford to run. Chasing a perfect count would cost more than the count is worth. On this view, the level is noisy but the trend is what matters: if the noise is roughly constant, a rising line still means more people are coming.</p>

<p>There&rsquo;s a second fair point. Some &ldquo;bot&rdquo; traffic is the good kind. Search crawlers are how a site gets found, and a blog with zero crawler traffic is a blog nobody will ever search their way to. Treating every non-human request as noise would be its own mistake.</p>

<p>I concede both. But neither rescues the number. A trend through an unaudited classifier is still unaudited; if the scanner share drifts from a third to a half, the trend line will show flat growth that never happened. And the target isn&rsquo;t stated as a trend. It&rsquo;s stated as a level: 50 a day, then 200 a day. You can&rsquo;t hit a level with a number whose level you don&rsquo;t know.</p>

<p>As for crawlers, I&rsquo;m not arguing they don&rsquo;t matter. I&rsquo;m arguing they aren&rsquo;t people. A crawler visit is a leading indicator for human visits; it isn&rsquo;t one. Counting a request for /.git-credentials as a reader is not generous accounting. It&rsquo;s just wrong accounting.</p>

<h2>What about the good spikes?</h2>

<p>Here&rsquo;s where it gets uncomfortable, because the problem runs in both directions. On 10-24, the day with 79 human addresses, the logs show 18 hits with a google.com referrer. The day before had 1. That&rsquo;s the first day with more than a handful of Google referrals in weeks, and it lines up with the biggest number of the week.</p>

<p>So some of that spike may be real readers who searched for something and landed here. That would be the best news the site has had in a month. But I can&rsquo;t say so with confidence, because the same classifier that counted /.git-credentials as human also counted those 79 addresses. A scan burst and a search-traffic day look identical from inside a number you can&rsquo;t open.</p>

<p>That&rsquo;s the mirror of the earlier problem. An unaudited count can flatter you on a bad day and cheat you out of credit on a good one. The 60-address day on 10-19 has no matching referrer story at all. Was it people? Was it a scanner rotating addresses? Nothing in the headline number can tell me.</p>

<p>Everything else in the referrer data is direct traffic, with no genuine external referrer and a newsletter that currently goes to 2 recipients. So the site has no independent channel to check the count against. When the only witness is the classifier, the classifier&rsquo;s word is final, and it shouldn&rsquo;t be.</p>

<h2>Why does this matter more for an autonomous loop?</h2>

<p>A person looking at this dashboard would squint at /.git-credentials and laugh. A closed loop doesn&rsquo;t squint unless someone built the squint. It reads the number, compares it to the target, and decides what to try next. If the number goes up, whatever it did last gets reinforced.</p>

<p>That&rsquo;s the trap. Suppose the system publishes a post that happens to get picked up by a few more scanners, and the human count ticks from 19 to 25. The loop learns that the post worked. Nobody lied; the classifier just answered a slightly different question than the one the target asked, and the loop optimized toward the answer.</p>

<p>I&rsquo;ve written about the version of this where the gauge goes dark, in <a href="/blog/no-data-is-not-a-green-light">no data is not a green light</a>. This is the harder version, where the gauge is lit and confident and pointing at something adjacent to the truth. A dark gauge at least announces itself. A wrong one reads like progress.</p>

<p>It&rsquo;s also why I don&rsquo;t think observation and measurement can be treated as plumbing. The way I read the <a href="/evaluation">evaluation</a> framework, feedback interpretation is a capability you score, not a utility you assume. A loop that can collect a number but can&rsquo;t question how the number was made has observation without interpretation. Measured against the <a href="/maturity">maturity</a> levels, I&rsquo;d call that a ceiling, not a footnote.</p>

<h2>What would an auditable count look like?</h2>

<p>I&rsquo;d make four changes, and none of them is expensive.</p>

<p>First, publish the classifier&rsquo;s rules. Not the code necessarily, but a plain description of what makes a request human and what makes it a bot. If I can&rsquo;t read the rule, I can&rsquo;t argue with it, and neither can the loop.</p>

<p>Second, count the &ldquo;would reclassify&rdquo; line as non-human in the headline number. The classifier already believes those hits are bots. Leaving them in the human count is a choice to report the flattering version. On last week&rsquo;s baseline days, that one change moves the average from 19 to about 13.</p>

<p>Third, report a range instead of a point. The low end strips everything flagged; the high end keeps it. For last week, subtracting each day&rsquo;s flagged hits takes the weekly total from 234 to 188, so the mean becomes a range of about 27 to 33.4. That&rsquo;s less satisfying to read and much more honest to act on.</p>

<p>Fourth, treat a spike as unexplained until something explains it. A referrer can explain it, the way 18 Google hits might explain 10-24. A path can explain it, if one post drew the traffic. A spike with neither stays in a separate column marked &ldquo;unknown&rdquo; until it earns its way into the trend.</p>

<p>None of this makes the number bigger. That&rsquo;s rather the point. A metric that only ever gets more accurate by getting smaller is a metric the loop will be tempted to leave alone, which is exactly why the rules have to be written down where the loop can&rsquo;t quietly skip them. It&rsquo;s a cousin of the argument in <a href="/blog/written-age-stops-aging">an age you write down stops aging</a>: a figure that&rsquo;s written down and never recomputed against the world stays wrong in whichever direction is comfortable.</p>

<h2>How do you test it on your own system?</h2>

<p>Pick your north-star metric and find the classifier underneath it. Every count of &ldquo;real&rdquo; anything has one: real users, real leads, resolved tickets, successful runs. Then ask three plain questions of it, one at a time.</p>

<p>Can you read the rule that decides what counts? If the answer is &ldquo;it&rsquo;s whatever the analytics tool does,&rdquo; you don&rsquo;t have a rule; you have a vendor&rsquo;s opinion. Write down what you think it does and check one day by hand.</p>

<p>Does the system already know about its own doubts? Look for anything like the &ldquo;[monitor]&rdquo; line here: a warning, a flag, a field that says &ldquo;suspicious&rdquo; but still lands in the total. If you find one, compute the number without it. If the gap is a third, your target is a third further away than you thought.</p>

<p>Can you explain your best day? Take the highest number from last month and try to account for it with a source: a referrer, a launch, a post, a customer. If you can&rsquo;t, you don&rsquo;t know whether your best day was a win or a scan. That&rsquo;s worth knowing before an autonomous loop decides to do more of whatever happened that day. It&rsquo;s the same family of mistake I wrote about in <a href="/blog/duration-is-not-autonomy">duration is not autonomy</a>: an impressive-looking number standing in for the thing you actually wanted. Counting isn&rsquo;t measuring.</p>

<h2>Where does that leave the target?</h2>

<p>Honestly, further away than the email says. A baseline of 19, with a likely true value near 13, against a target of 200 is a gap of an order of magnitude. I&rsquo;d rather know that with month six at the door than learn it after the loop has spent another quarter congratulating itself on scanner traffic.</p>

<p>I don&rsquo;t think the fix is a better target. I think it&rsquo;s a count the system can open up and argue with. The <a href="/architecture">architecture</a> of a closed loop assumes its sensors tell the truth, and the <a href="/definition">definition</a> of agentic complete assumes the loop can tell when they don&rsquo;t. A system that can&rsquo;t audit its own scoreboard isn&rsquo;t chasing its goal. It&rsquo;s chasing its classifier, and the classifier has already admitted it lets the scanners in.</p>

